Exercise 4.14 asks whether a monotone endomap \(f : PA \to PA\) must have a maximum fixed point, and the formalization answers yes. `gfpSet` is the union of the post-fixed points \(\{x \mid x \subseteq f(x)\}\). `subset_gfpSet` places every post-fixed point in that union. `gfpSet_subset_f` shows the union is post-fixed: an element belongs to some post-fixed \(x\), that \(x\) is contained in the union, and monotonicity puts the element in the image of the union. `gfpSet_isFixed` then observes that monotonicity makes the image post-fixed, so the image is contained in the union, and the two inclusions agree. `gfpSet_greatest` contains every fixed point in the union, because a fixed point equals its image and is therefore post-fixed. `example_414` is the conjunction of that comparison and the fixed-point equation, so the union is a fixed point lying above every fixed point. The same development defines `lfpSet` as the intersection of the pre-fixed points and proves `f_subset_lfpSet`, `lfpSet_isFixed`, and `lfpSet_least`; that is the power-set form of the least-fixed-point formula and leaves the greatest-fixed-point claim unchanged. **Theorem:** yes — a monotone endomap of a power set has a maximum fixed point, the union of its post-fixed points. **Proof:** yes — post-fixedness of the union, the reverse inclusion by monotonicity, and the comparison with an arbitrary fixed point are the greatest-fixed-point argument.
